Picture a revenue line that has not moved in three years. Rs 32 crore, then Rs 33 crore, then Rs 31 crore. The sales team is busy, the plant runs two shifts, and the founder works longer hours than he did at Rs 15 crore. If your **MSME business is not growing** and that picture feels familiar, you have probably tried the obvious fixes already: push sales harder, open a new territory, hire a senior manager.

A Rs 10–50 crore business that stops growing is sitting on one of four different plateaus, and all four look the same on the revenue line. It stays stuck because the founder picks the fix that suits his instinct, not the one that suits his plateau. Each plateau has a favourite wrong fix, and that fix usually makes it worse.

---

## Why is my MSME not growing? The plateau looks the same from the top

Start with where this band sits. Since 1 April 2025, the revised government definition caps a micro enterprise at Rs 10 crore of turnover, and a small enterprise runs up to Rs 100 crore. A business between Rs 10 crore and Rs 50 crore has left micro behind and sits in the lower half of small. It has proved its product, its customers and its founder. What it has not yet proved is that it can keep growing once the conditions that got it here run out.

Very few Indian firms make that step. In a 2014 paper in the *Quarterly Journal of Economics*, the economists Chang-Tai Hsieh and Peter Klenow compared how manufacturing plants grow with age. In the United States, the average 40-year-old plant employs more than seven times as many workers as a plant five years old or younger. Surviving plants in India and Mexico only roughly double over the same span. Economists call the result India's **missing middle**: plenty of small firms, very few mid-sized ones.

Flat is also less safe than it looks. Revenue is counted in rupees of the day. If your prices and costs rise by around 5% a year, three flat years leave you with roughly 14% less real business than you started with. A plateau is a slow decline that the top line hides.

So when a founder asks "why is my MSME not growing?", he is usually staring at one number. Revenue works like a thermometer. It tells you there is a fever. It cannot tell you whether the cause is an infection, dehydration or something worse, and treating every fever with the same tablet is how a mild problem becomes a serious one.

---

## Four kinds of business growth plateau at Rs 10 crore and above

In my experience, nearly every MSME business not growing in this band is on one of four plateaus, and many are on two at once. Each leaves a different mark in the numbers. Each tempts the founder into a different mistake.

### 1. The leaking plateau

**What it looks like:** Order volumes hold, enquiries keep coming, but gross margin slides and repeat customers drift away. The business wins about as much each year as it quietly loses.

**The tempting fix:** A new state or a new market. If the home market feels tired, go somewhere fresh.

**Why it backfires:** The new market inherits the same unreviewed discounts, the same quotes nobody follows up and the same lapsed customers nobody calls. You pay for growth twice and keep half of it.

**The first move:** Find the leaks in your invoice data before you add anything. I have set out [a ten-point audit for a business that has stopped growing](https://www.simpleworks.in/blog/business-not-growing-a-business-growth-consultant-s-audit), starting with price realisation and dormant accounts.

### 2. The narrow plateau

**What it looks like:** Margins are healthy and the business is well run, but revenue rests on a small base, such as a handful of customers, one region or one channel, and that base has stopped growing. You are not losing. You have sold everything that base will buy.

**The tempting fix:** Discounts and schemes to squeeze more volume out of the customers you already have.

**Why it backfires:** Large customers learn that pressure works. A supplier who cannot afford to lose its biggest buyer cannot hold its price with that buyer either, so margin falls and the base stays the same size. I wrote about this trap for auto component makers in [customer concentration risk in Hosur's auto belt](https://www.simpleworks.in/blog/when-one-oem-is-60-of-your-revenue-customer-concentration-risk-in-hosur-s-auto-component-belt).

**The first move:** Build a second leg on purpose: a new segment, channel or geography. Give it its own owner and its own budget, rather than adding it to the existing sales team's list of duties.

### 3. The cash-starved plateau

**What it looks like:** The business is profitable on paper, yet every new order needs more working capital than the last. Receivable days creep up, stock builds, and the bank limit is always full. Growth stops because the business cannot fund it.

**The tempting fix:** A bigger loan and more inventory, "so we never miss an order".

**Why it backfires:** Borrowed money feeds the same slow collections and the same excess stock. The interest bill grows, the plateau stays, and the founder spends his week managing the bank instead of the business.

**The first move:** Bring working-capital days down before you ask for higher limits. Shorter credit terms are easier to argue for than many suppliers think. Since financial year 2023–24, Section 43B(h) of the Income Tax Act has meant that a buyer who pays a Udyam-registered micro or small manufacturer or service provider later than 15 days (or 45 days with a written agreement) cannot claim that expense for tax until the year it actually pays.

### 4. The founder-bound plateau

**What it looks like:** Demand is there and cash is fine, but decisions queue up at one desk. Pricing exceptions, credit approvals, hiring and the important customer calls all wait for the founder. Revenue stops at whatever one person can attend to in a week.

**The tempting fix:** Hire a senior general manager or a sales head.

**Why it backfires:** The new hire arrives, but the decisions stay where they were. The hire struggles in a role with no real authority, leaves within a year or two, and the founder concludes that good people are not available in his town.

**The first move:** Hand over decisions before you hand over titles. I have argued that [the revenue ceiling is often the founder's own calendar](https://www.simpleworks.in/blog/how-indian-msm-es-can-break-through-the-10-year-revenue-ceiling), and the practical steps are in [fixing founder dependency, decisions before the org chart](https://www.simpleworks.in/blog/founder-dependency-fix-the-decisions-before-the-org-chart).

---

## Stuck at Rs 10–50 crore revenue? Read four lines before you act

You can tell which plateau you are on in an afternoon, without any software. Put the last twelve quarters on one page and draw four lines next to revenue.

1. **Gross margin percentage.** If revenue is flat and margin is falling, you are leaking.
1. **Working-capital days**, meaning receivable days plus inventory days, minus payable days. If this climbs while revenue stays flat, you are cash-starved.
1. **Share of revenue from your top five customers**, and from your largest region or channel. If this share is high and those customers have stopped growing, you are narrow.
1. **Founder load.** For two weeks, list every decision that waited for you. If the list is long and getting longer, you are founder-bound.

Most businesses find two lines moving at once. A narrow business often leaks as well, because its big customers have learned to win discounts. A founder-bound business is often cash-starved, because only the founder chases the large overdue accounts.

When two plateaus stack, the order of repair matters. Fix the leak first, because it is the cheapest money in the business and it pays for everything else. Fix cash second, because you cannot grow what you cannot finance. Free the founder third. Widen a narrow base last. It is the most expensive move, and it fails when a leaking, cash-starved business run by one exhausted person attempts it.

None of this involves a new brand, a new market or a new office. Every step is inside your control, and each one makes the next one cheaper.

---

## The strongest objection: is a plateau at Rs 10–50 crore so bad?

The best case against this argument has two parts.

First, a steady, profitable Rs 30 crore business can be the right outcome. Many Indian family businesses have run at one size for decades, paid their people well and given their owners a good life. Not every founder needs to build a Rs 300 crore company, and constant pressure to grow has damaged plenty of good businesses.

Second, the Indian environment makes growth costly. Labour and compliance obligations step up as headcount crosses thresholds. Credit is hardest to find exactly when a firm outgrows small-business lending. Large buyers pay late. The missing middle is partly a policy outcome, not a founder's failing.

Both points are fair, and I would not try to talk a founder out of a size he has chosen. But there is a simple test of whether a plateau is a choice or a drift: are margin and working-capital days holding steady? If they are, you have a stable business, and the four lines will show it. If margin is sliding or cash days are rising, the plateau is not a resting place. It is a decline that inflation makes harder to see.

On the environment, some of the fear is out of date. Since 2022, the Ministry of MSME has let an enterprise that moves up a category keep the non-tax benefits of its old category for three years. The constraints that remain fall on every competitor in your sector, and some of those competitors still grow.

There is one real limit. If your whole category is shrinking because customers are switching to a different technology or product, none of the four fixes will save you. That is a fifth situation, and the answer is to move into something new rather than to tune what you have.

---

## Ask which plateau you are on, not how to grow

When the revenue line flattens, the natural question is "how do we grow?" It sends founders straight to the fix they already like: more selling, a new market, more capital, a senior hire. Each of those is the right answer for one plateau and the wrong answer for the other three.

The better question is "which plateau are we on?" It is slower to ask and it does not feel like action. But a founder who can name his plateau has already stopped paying for the wrong fix, and that is often the first real progress in years.

So before your next planning meeting, draw the four lines. If your MSME business is not growing, they will tell you why far more honestly than the revenue line can. If you want a second pair of eyes on them, that is the purpose of the Simpleworks [Business Growth Review](https://www.simpleworks.in/business-growth-review).

